Date: Thu, 19 Aug 2027 14:23:00 -0700
Subject: Re: Staging -> Prod traffic migration: SDK retry & rate-safety plan

Samir — answers inline and a drop-in snippet you can run in QA. Attaching the runbook and a small diff of our SDK config.

1) Token expiry during in-flight requests: we recommend the client (your gateway) include token expiry metadata on each request (x-token-expiry-unix). If a request hits 401 with body code TOKEN_EXPIRED, do one immediate synchronous refresh and replay the request once (use the same idempotency key). If refresh fails, propagate 401 to caller. This prevents infinite retry loops.

2) Duplicate webhook deliveries: Redwood's hosted runtime will retry idempotent requests but we still advise server-side de-dup. Our guarantee is "at-least-once" for retries; use x-idempotency-key to make create operations idempotent server-side for safety. For webhooks, include a dedupe id in the payload.

3) Rate headers: Redwood prefers Retry-After when present. If only X-Rate-Limit-Remaining and X-Rate-Limit-Reset exist we compute delay = max(1, X-Rate-Limit-Reset - now). If both are present, Retry-After takes precedence. We'll add a small QA check that simulates missing Retry-After to validate your header set.

Minimal SDK snippet (Node, drop-in):

const fetch = require('node-fetch');
const uuidv4 = require('uuid').v4;

async function requestWithRetries(url, body, opts = {}) {
  const maxRetries = opts.retries || 4;
  const base = opts.backoffBaseMs || 250;
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= maxRetries; attempt++) {
    try {
      const res = await fetch(url, {
        method: 'POST',
        body: JSON.stringify(body),
        headers: {
          'Content-Type': 'application/json',
          'Authorization': `Bearer ${process.env.AURORA_TOKEN}`,
          'X-Idempotency-Key': body.idempotency || uuidv4()
        },
        timeout: opts.timeout || 15000
      });

      if (res.status === 429) {
        const ra = res.headers.get('retry-after');
        const delay = ra ? parseInt(ra, 10) * 1000 : Math.min(base * Math.pow(2, attempt), 60000);
        await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, delay * (1 + (Math.random() * (opts.jitter || 0.25)))));
        continue;
      }

      if (res.status >= 500) throw new Error('server-error');

      return await res.json();
    } catch (err) {
      if (attempt === maxRetries) throw err;
      const delay = Math.min(base * Math.pow(2, attempt), 60000);
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, delay * (1 + (Math.random() * (opts.jitter || 0.25)))));
    }
  }
}

Notes we added to the runbook:
- Endpoint migration map: staging: api-staging.aurorapay.com -> prod: api.aurorapay.com; update DNS TTL to 60s for quick rollback.
- Batch sizes: our runtime suggests batches of 8–12 for embedding calls to balance latency/cost; streaming calls should be single-request flows.
- Concurrency: recommend starting with concurrency 4 per worker and ramp to 12 if latency stable.
- KV cache warmup: pre-warm most frequent prefixes for 30 minutes prior to canary to avoid cold start tail latency.

I've attached aurorapay-staging-runbook.pdf (sequence + monitoring playbook) and sdk-config-examples.diff with JSON config you can apply to QA. Also added HubSpot task HS-10293847 for the cutover checklist.

